 ## Background
-The concept of optimizing vertex positions to reduce geometric error in surface extraction has evolved from simple linear interpolation [4447-marching-cubes-vertex-list-generation.md] to more sophisticated error minimization techniques. Position Error Minimized Marching Cubes (PEM-MC) introduced vertex snapping to the isosurface [7435-position-error-minimized-marching-cubes.md]. Edge Transformations (Macet) addressed mesh quality by moving edge endpoints along gradients and tangents [10929-edge-transformations-for-improving-mesh-quality-of-marching-.md]. QEF-based optimization applies quadratic error functions to resolve ambiguities and refine positions [3831-marching-cubes-with-qef-vertex-optimization.md]. Midpoint Marching Cubes offers a simplified, non-interpolating alternative [12551-midpoint-marching-cubes.md]. In the context of differentiable rendering and neural reconstruction, Laplacian smoothing and normal consistency are used as regularization losses to prevent mesh distortion during joint optimization with Gaussian Splatting [S1]. Additionally, post-processing filters like the Fix Position Filter are used to cancel positional noise in registered data sequences [S2].
